on\":\"click\",\"values\":false}},\"options\":{\"raster\":false,\"navigator\":false,\"navigatorHeight\":null,\"toolbar\":true,\"hoverMode\":\"closest\",\"crosshair\":false,\"legendClick\":\"select\",\"tooltipDelay\":0,\"tooltipFollow\":true,\"tooltipSticky\":false,\"a11y\":true,\"alt\":null,\"selectMode\":\"multiple\",\"group\":null,\"crosstalk\":null,\"style\":[],\"export\":{}},\"provenance\":{\"on\":\"click\",\"byId\":{\"layer-1-bar-g1\":[1,4,6],\"layer-1-bar-g2\":[2,5,7],\"layer-1-bar-g3\":[3,8]},\"fields\":[\"cyl\",\"gear\",\"mpg\",\"id\"]}},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nClicking a bar shows the rows it was computed from, in a small popover. Under the\nhood the widget answers from the compiled scene's provenance rather than from a\nlookup you had to build: each grob knows which input rows it came from. Under Shiny\nthe same gesture sets `input$<id>_source`, and in plain HTML it fires a bubbling\n`vellum:source` DOM event you can listen for. Pass `inspect_source(values = TRUE)`\nto ship the row *values* too, at the cost of a heavier payload.\n\nOne more thing worth mentioning because it is invisible when it works: when a plot\nhas too many keyed marks to ship as per-element SVG, the widget draws the scene\nonce as a raster and runs interactions off a compact index — and that raster is now\nrendered at 2× and encoded in memory, so it is sharp on a retina screen without\ntouching disk. The bounding boxes and panel rectangles stay in device pixels, so\nnothing about picking or pan/zoom changes. See\n[A big scatter that stays interactive](big-interactive.qmd) for that path at\n120,000 points.\n",
